# tools/cr/crypto_fe_secp256k1_witness.rish — the secp256k1 base field GF(2^256 − 2^32 − 977) authored in pure Rye: the durable stone the whole
# secp256k1 tower (group law, ECDSA verify, public-key recovery) stands on, the crux the Bitcoin/Ethereum address arc was climbing toward.
# Proven against by-hand known-answers and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, parity-checked, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-020351_fe-secp256k1-the-bitcoin-ethereum-field-crux.md (this round) and
# active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the priority spine: Rye first, Glow on green Rye).
# Kin: crypto/fe25519.rye (the sibling field — five 51-bit limbs mod 2^255 − 19; this is eight 32-bit limbs mod 2^256 − 2^32 − 977) ·
# crypto/eth_address.rye · crypto/keccak256.rye. secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so its source parity is not a rung here; the RFC/reference
# known-answers plus Zig's independent std.crypto are the two witnesses (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye carries a field element as eight 32-bit limbs (radix 2^32) mod p = 2^256 − 2^32 − 977, so the reduction boundary
# 2^256 lands exactly on a limb edge: limbwise add and sub, a schoolbook multiply with the 2^256 ≡ R fold (R = 2^32 + 977), squaring as the
# product with itself, and Fermat's inverse a^(p−2) by a fixed 256-bit square-and-multiply ladder; packing performs the final conditional
# subtract of p so equal values always serialize identically. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against two
# references runnable now: by-hand known-answers (1·1 = 1, 2·3 = 6, 1 round-trips, a − a = 0, a·a⁻¹ = 1, and the encodings of p and p+5
# reduce to 0 and 5), and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.Fe as an independent Montgomery-domain implementation, byte-for-byte on the
# canonical big-endian packing of add, sub, mul, sq, and invert across a deterministic spread of twenty-four field elements. Purely local —
# a field element is arithmetic, not a secret and not a signature: no real key, no signing (ECDSA signing stays its own gated round), no
# network, no funds, no real device; constant-time timing-safety stays a separate named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_fe_secp256k1_witness.rish

say "crypto-fe_secp256k1: Language EN — the secp256k1 base field GF(2^256 − 2^32 − 977) authored in pure Rye. The recent Season G arc built the Bitcoin/Ethereum address layer whole — Base58Check, Bech32, Keccak-256, RIPEMD-160, EIP-55 — each rung climbing toward the one place it could not reach: the signature scheme both chains sign with, ECDSA over secp256k1. eth_address derives an address FROM a public key; nothing yet derives that key or verifies a signature, because the curve beneath it was never built. This is that curve's durable stone — pure arithmetic, no key, no signature, no custody gate — so once it is parity-GREEN, the group law and ECDSA become composition rounds over a proven base."
say "crypto-fe_secp256k1: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a field element is a residue mod p = 2^256 − 2^32 − 977 carried in eight 32-bit limbs, chosen so the reduction boundary 2^256 lands exactly on a limb edge; add and subtract limbwise, multiply with the field's own fold (any weight past 2^256 returns multiplied by R = 2^32 + 977, because 2^256 ≡ R), square as the product with itself, invert by Fermat's a^(p−2), and pack with a final conditional subtract of p so equal values always serialize identically — the property the byte-for-byte parity check depends on."
say "crypto-fe_secp256k1: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it bounds every column below the multiply schedule's headroom, accumulates products in 128-bit words, and asserts its contracts on every function. Parity holds against by-hand known-answers AND Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.Fe byte-for-byte on the canonical big-endian packing of add, sub, mul, sq, and invert; because the check is on canonical bytes, the two implementations carry limbs their own way and still agree exactly. secp256k1 is not in Monocypher, so the two witnesses are the reference known-answers and Zig's std.crypto. Purely local — a field element is not a secret and not a signature, so no real key, no signing, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays a separate named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/fe_secp256k1"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/fe_secp256k1.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-fe_secp256k1: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/fe_secp256k1.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-fe_secp256k1: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-fe_secp256k1: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-fe_secp256k1' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-fe_secp256k1: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-fe_secp256k1 line"
